  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 958 PM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nWestern areas will remain favored for storms tonight and on \nMonday as the monsoon high shifts east over the mid-MS River \nValley. CAMs show isolated to scattered convection redeveloping\novernight along the AZ-NM border before pushing north by Mon AM.\nPWs increase a bit on Mon area-wide (1-1.1\" for KELP) as the \nheavy rain threat persists W of the Divide, mainly in the\nBootheel. There is some forcing tomorrow with a shortwave moving\ninto the Four Corners within SW flow aloft. Isolated showers and\nstorms are forecast during the afternoon for much of the \nlowlands, including El Paso and Las Cruces, with drier conditions \nout east. A sfc convergence zone looks to develop in the evening \nnear the RGV (better SE flow to the east and W flow to the west).\nThis could spark scattered showers and storms Mon night as \nreflected by several HRRR runs, progressing northward before\nfizzling Tue AM.\n\nGlobal models are in agreement with the upper high focusing over the \nCarolinas/TN Valley through midweek and stretching across the \nsouthern Plains, keeping much of the monsoonal moisture south and\nwest of us. Low mtn storm chances are forecast from Tue onward as\nthe lowlands stay mainly dry. Western areas will be favored for\nstorms and isolated heavy rainfall through midweek. \n\nThe high looks to centralize over the central/southern Plains into\nnext weekend and reinvigorate the moisture plume. Areas to the \nwest appear best set up for storm chances and heavy rainfall to \nend the period. For much of the area, PWs look to be slightly\nabove normal for next weekend with potential introduction of\ntropical moisture from a Pacific system. The placement of the high\nwill determine how much moisture gets scooped up into the region,\nbut better rain chances overall are anticipated.\n\nTemps will be steady through the week with highs 4-8 degrees \nabove normal. Lowland highs of 95-100F are forecast each day with \nslight cooling on Tue behind the shortwave. Winds remain light \nthrough the week mainly from the W.",
